<div class="page">
  <div class="section-title">RESEARCH</div>
  <p><span data-field="research_summary">{{ research_summary }}</span></p>
  <p><span data-field="research_summary_text">I have published peer-reviewed articles in leading journals including: Nature, Nature Neurosci., Mol. Psychiatry, Brain, Annals Neurol., J. Neurosci., Cerebral Cortex, Neuropsychopharmacology, Hum. Mol. Genet., Neurobiol. Dis., Trans. Psychiatry, Mol. Autism, J. Physiol., Brain Behav. Immun., Nature Rev. Neurosci., Trends Neurosci., Trends Genet., Trends Mol. Med., Prog. Neurobiol., Neurosci. Biobehav. Rev., and Bioessays. I have also edited two books, one as sole editor. The journal articles have been cited <span data-field="citation_count">{{ research_summary_text">I have published peer-reviewed articles in leading journals including: Nature, Nature Neurosci., Mol. Psychiatry, Brain, Annals Neurol., J. Neurosci., Cerebral Cortex, Neuropsychopharmacology, Hum. Mol. Genet., Neurobiol. Dis., Trans. Psychiatry, Mol. Autism, J. Physiol., Brain Behav. Immun., Nature Rev. Neurosci., Trends Neurosci., Trends Genet., Trends Mol. Med., Prog. Neurobiol., Neurosci. Biobehav. Rev., and Bioessays. I have also edited two books, one as sole editor. The journal articles have been cited <span data-field= }}</span> (h-index > <span data-field="h_index">{{ h_index }}</span>).</span></p>

  <p><span data-field="research_summary_text_2">My research group has taken our original discovery, with collaborators in Oxford, demonstrating that environmental enrichment (EE) delays onset of motor symptoms in Huntington's disease (HD); van Dellen et al., 2000, Nature, <span data-field="citation_count_2">{{ research_summary_text_2">My research group has taken our original discovery, with collaborators in Oxford, demonstrating that environmental enrichment (EE) delays onset of motor symptoms in Huntington's disease (HD); van Dellen et al., 2000, Nature, <span data-field= }}</span>; Hockly et al., 2002, Annals Neurol., <span data-field="citation_count_3">{{ citation_count_3 }}</span> cites; Spires et al., 2004, J. Neurosci., <span data-field="citation_count_4">{{ citation_count_4 }}</span> cites) and demonstrated that EE also delays onset of cognitive (Nithanantharajah et al., 2008, Neurobiol. Dis., <span data-field="citation_count_5">{{ citation_count_5 }}</span> cites) and affective (Pang et al., 2009, Hum. Mol. Genet., <span data-field="citation_count_6">{{ citation_count_6 }}</span> cites) symptoms. We were the first group to demonstrate that an animal model of HD develops depressive-like symptoms (Grote et al., 2005, Eur. J. Neurosci., <span data-field="citation_count_7">{{ citation_count_7 }}</span> cites); Pang et al., 2009, Hum. Mol. Genet., <span data-field="citation_count_8">{{ citation_count_8 }}</span> cites). Furthermore, we were the first to demonstrate that increased voluntary physical exercise has beneficial effects in an animal model of HD (Pang et al., 2006, Neurosci., <span data-field="citation_count_9">{{ citation_count_9 }}</span> cites). Most recently we have discovered experience-dependent HPA-axis dysfunction in HD mice (Du et al., 2012, Transl. Psychiatry) and that chronic stress can accelerate neurodegeneration in HD mice (Mo et al., 2013, J. Neurosci.). We were the first to demonstrate that EE can ameliorate symptoms in animal models of schizophrenia (McComish et al., 2008, Mol. Psychiatry, <span data-field="citation_count_10">{{ citation_count_10 }}</span> cites) and Rett syndrome (an autism spectrum disorder; Kondo et al., 2008, <span data-field="citation_count_11">{{ citation_count_11 }}</span> cites). We also characterized two new mouse models of schizophrenia involving disrupted cortical development, cognition and behaviour and demonstrated face predictive learning in HD mice (van Dellen et al., 2001, Nature Neurosci., <span data-field="citation_count_12">{{ citation_count_12 }}</span> cites; Spires et al., 2005, Cerebral Cortex, <span data-field="citation_count_13">{{ citation_count_13 }}</span> cites; McComish et al., 2008, Mol. Psychiatry, <span data-field="citation_count_14">{{ citation_count_14 }}</span> cites; Gray et al., 2009, Int. J. Neuropsychopharmacology, <span data-field="citation_count_15">{{ citation_count_15 }}</span> cites; Burrows et al., 2015, Neuropsychopharmacology).</span></p>

  <p><span data-field="research_summary_text_3">This has led to invited plenaries at international and national meetings and invited reviews in major journals where new concepts have been proposed, including that of “environmental” drugs which could mimic or enhance the beneficial effects of environmental stimulation (e.g. Nithanantharajah and Hannan, 2006, Nature Rev. Neurosci., <span data-field="citation_count_16">{{ research_summary_text_3">This has led to invited plenaries at international and national meetings and invited reviews in major journals where new concepts have been proposed, including that of “environmental” drugs which could mimic or enhance the beneficial effects of environmental stimulation (e.g. Nithanantharajah and Hannan, 2006, Nature Rev. Neurosci., <span data-field= }}</span> cites). Our discoveries in HD mice have led to policy to studies at the University of Melbourne demonstrating a role for environmental factors in clinical HD (Trembath et al., 2010, Mov. Dis.). I have also contributed to our understanding of tandem repeat polymorphisms and their health and disease (Nithanantharajah and Hannan, 2007, Bioessays, <span data-field="citation_count_17">{{ citation_count_17 }}</span> cites; Fondon et al., 2008, Trends Neurosci., <span data-field="citation_count_18">{{ citation_count_18 }}</span> cites; Hannan, 2010, Trends Genet., <span data-field="citation_count_19">{{ citation_count_19 }}</span> cites).</span></p>

  <p><span data-field="research_summary_text_4">Our work demonstrating that environmental modifiers can exert major effects in HD mice is being translated into clinical trials and practice by various groups, including the European Huntington's Disease Network (EHDN) Environmental Modifiers Working Group, of which I am a member. A Melbourne clinician researcher, Prof. Martin Delatinky, whose group published the first epidemiological study translating our work in HD mice (Trembath et al., 2010, Mov. Dis.), was an instigator of this important translational research. I have been invited by the Cure Huntington's Disease Initiative (CHDI), largest international HD philanthropy to a workshop (<span data-field="workshop_count">{{ research_summary_text_4">Our work demonstrating that environmental modifiers can exert major effects in HD mice is being translated into clinical trials and practice by various groups, including the European Huntington's Disease Network (EHDN) Environmental Modifiers Working Group, of which I am a member. A Melbourne clinician researcher, Prof. Martin Delatinky, whose group published the first epidemiological study translating our work in HD mice (Trembath et al., 2010, Mov. Dis.), was an instigator of this important translational research. I have been invited by the Cure Huntington's Disease Initiative (CHDI), largest international HD philanthropy to a workshop (<span data-field= }}</span> participants) in New York dedicated to translating our findings on environmental enrichment and exercise into epidemiological studies and intervention trials, which are now being conducted internationally. We have also recently identified a new therapeutic in HD mice (Wright et al., 2015, Transl. Psychiatry) and clinical colleagues are keen to take this drug into a clinical trial.</span></p>
</div>

